00:06:18,553 --> 00:06:19,354
Story time.
故事时间。

79
00:06:19,374 --> 00:06:27,580
And the first thing that comes to my mind whenever I was thinking about this was the story of when I was an intern at Insert Big Tech Company here, like two summers ago.
每当我想到这个话题时，脑海中浮现的第一件事就是我在这里插入大科技公司的实习经历，大约是两年前的夏天。

80
00:06:28,040 --> 00:06:40,890
And I was so, so proud of writing this piece of code that was like this recursive structure that could take any length array of any different lengths arrays, and it would give you all the permutations of all the items.
我为写出这段代码感到非常自豪，这是一种递归结构，可以处理任何长度的不同长度数组，并生成所有项目的排列组合。

81
00:06:41,270 --> 00:06:43,672
And I was so stupidly proud of this
我为此感到愚蠢地自豪。

82
00:06:44,693 --> 00:06:47,714
because I thought it was the best thing that anyone had ever written and that I had written.
因为我觉得这是任何人写过的最好的代码，而我也是其中之一。

83
00:06:47,974 --> 00:06:53,156
And I got even more stupidly proud of it when it got approved and the code went through and the code is in the code base now.
当它获得批准，代码通过审核并被纳入代码库时，我的自豪感更加愚蠢了。

84
00:06:53,897 --> 00:07:00,319
And then I came back a year later as a full-time employee, and I immediately realized why it was so dumb of me to be proud of that.
然后我一年后作为全职员工回来了，立刻意识到我为此感到自豪是多么愚蠢。

85
00:07:00,439 --> 00:07:06,662
And in fact, why it's actually insane that the code got approved in the first place, because I was looking at it and I was like, I have no idea what this is.
事实上，代码第一次获得批准是多么疯狂，因为我看着它，心想，我根本不知道这段代码在做什么。

86
00:07:07,082 --> 00:07:12,125
And it's not because previous me was smarter, it was because it was just a really messy piece of code, right?
这并不是因为之前的我更聪明，而是因为这段代码实在太混乱了，对吧？

87
00:07:12,465 --> 00:07:18,709
It was something that sure might've been smart, but if nobody can read and understand it, when it breaks, it's just broken forever and it's useless.
这段代码可能确实聪明，但如果没有人能阅读和理解它，当它崩溃时，它就会永远崩溃，毫无用处。

88
00:07:19,130 --> 00:07:29,656
Nevermind somebody else, like a different engineer coming back and doing it, will you, like your own human being two days from now, will you be able to look at this and like work on it and fix it, right?
更别提其他人，比如其他工程师，能否在两天后回过头来理解并修复它，对吧？

89
00:07:29,696 --> 00:07:33,679
Because if you don't know what's going on, God knows other people are not gonna know what's going on.
因为如果你不知道发生了什么，天知道其他人会知道发生了什么。

90
00:07:34,099 --> 00:07:54,140
And I think this really underscores my point about readability, where the microseconds that you might get from making an algorithm that much more efficient or that many less lines is almost never worth the pain and time wasted that you will have in the future or somebody else will have in the future trying to understand it, improve it, fix it, or change it.
我认为这确实强调了我关于可读性的观点，为了让算法更高效或减少几行代码而节省的微秒，几乎从来都不值得将来你或其他人试图理解、改进、修复或更改它所浪费的痛苦和时间。

91
00:07:54,560 --> 00:07:56,923
